       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the safety problem. Sertraline 1000 mg is five times the maximum dose of 200 mg/day. You haven't described any clinical reason for it, such as a supervised overdose-treatment protocol, and I don't know of one that would apply to a routine once-daily order. In a patient with normal kidney and liver function, it would be expected to cause harm.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** Tell me the dose, for example 50 mg or 100 mg by mouth once daily, and I'll enter it.\n- **Have your attending or resident review it.** If they intend a dose above the maximum, they can place or co-sign the order themselves. They can also involve pharmacy or toxicology if needed.\n- **Add a note.** I can enter a corrected order with a note such as \"dose verified with attending.\"\n\nIf the 1000 mg came from a chart, a sign-out, or a verbal instruction, please check it against the source. A transcription error is the most likely explanation."
      }
